# HOW THIS FILE IS RENDERED, NOW THAT THERE IS NO C BUILD
#
# This is the pkg-config template. Autotools substituted it through
# AC_SUBST and config.status; CMake substitutes it with configure_file()
# in @ONLY mode at CMakeLists.txt:2326-2328. A Cargo build runs neither,
# so the renderer is now curl-rs-ffi/build.rs: it reads this file, fills
# in the fourteen substitution tokens and writes lib/pkgconfig/libcurl.pc,
# mode 0644, into an install-staging directory under target/ -- or wherever
# CURL_RS_STAGING_DIR names, and never into the checkout.
#
# The token spelling is deliberately unchanged. Both CMake's
# configure_file() and the new renderer understand it, so a packager
# diffing this template against curl 8.x sees added comments and nothing
# else. Those comments therefore name each token WITHOUT its surrounding
# at-signs: substitution is a plain global replacement in both renderers,
# so a real token written inside a comment would be replaced there too.
#
# The pkg-config contract is frozen, not merely preserved. Every field
# name below, and the order of the fields, is the one curl 8.x published.
# Consumers run `pkg-config --libs libcurl`, `--cflags libcurl`,
# `--modversion libcurl`, `--variable=supported_protocols libcurl` and
# `--variable=supported_features libcurl`, and every one of them must keep
# answering in the same shape now that a Rust library stands behind it.
#
# The install layout. prefix is the only externally supplied value: the
# renderer reads CURL_RS_PREFIX and otherwise falls back to the same
# /usr/local Autotools defaulted to. The other three are pkg-config
# variable references resolved against it, so relocating the package needs
# no edit here. Only that namespaced variable is consulted -- a bare
# PREFIX inherited from an unrelated tool would silently describe install
# paths libcurl was never installed to.
prefix=@prefix@
exec_prefix=@exec_prefix@
libdir=@libdir@
includedir=@includedir@
# The two variables a consumer reads back with `pkg-config --variable=`.
#
# SUPPORT_PROTOCOLS expands to the schemes this implementation really
# serves, space separated and upper-cased exactly as curl 8.x spells them
# here: FILE FTP FTPS HTTP HTTPS SCP SFTP WS WSS. Those nine are the whole
# of it. The twenty-four schemes that remain registered for ABI
# completeness but are not implemented -- DICT, GOPHER, GOPHERS, IMAP,
# IMAPS, LDAP, LDAPS, MQTT, POP3, POP3S, RTMP and its variants, RTSP, SMB,
# SMBS, SMTP, SMTPS, TELNET and TFTP -- are deliberately absent, and that
# absence is the point rather than an oversight: they are out of scope for
# implementation but not for the ABI, so their absence has to be reported
# honestly. The asymmetry decides it. Under-reporting a capability only
# makes a test fixture skip, while over-reporting makes it run and fail,
# and 283 fixtures -- 14.8% of the corpus -- depend on this omission to
# skip cleanly. A request for one of them returns
# CURLE_UNSUPPORTED_PROTOCOL.
#
# SUPPORT_FEATURES expands to the truthful subset of curl's own feature
# vocabulary, each name spelled as configure.ac spells it from :5143
# onward and the list sorted case-insensitively as CMakeLists.txt:2040-2045
# sorts it: SSL, IPv6, UnixSockets, libz, brotli, zstd, AsynchDNS, IDN,
# PSL, alt-svc, HSTS, NTLM, HTTP2, HTTP3, Largefile, threadsafe and their
# neighbours, each one only when this build genuinely has it.
#
# Withheld on purpose, because each one would be a claim a test harness
# then acts on:
#
#   Debug, and with it TrackMemory -- advertising Debug arms the harness's
#   allocation-cap and leak accounting against a Rust allocator whose
#   allocation pattern cannot match a C one. The cost of withholding it is
#   stated rather than hidden: 98 fixtures skip and torture-test does not
#   apply.
#
#   Every name belonging to a dropped backend, since rustls, quinn and
#   russh are the sole implementations: MultiSSL, OpenSSL, GnuTLS,
#   mbedtls, wolfssl, Schannel, SSPI, TLS-SRP, NTLM_WB, WinIDN, AppleIDN,
#   libssh, libssh2, c-ares and gsasl.
#
#   GSS-API, SPNEGO and Kerberos, which are compiled in only behind the
#   non-default `negotiate` Cargo feature -- and even then stay out of
#   this file, because whether a usable mechanism exists on the host is a
#   runtime property that a file written at build time cannot know. That
#   question is left to `curl --version`, which can ask.
#
# Neither list is maintained here, and neither is maintained in the
# renderer. Both are projections of the one capability table in
# curl-rs-lib/src/version.rs that also renders the `curl --version`
# banner, so this file and that banner cannot describe different products
# -- which matters because tests/runtests.pl parses the banner's
# Protocols: line and derives the -ipv6 and -unix variants, http-proxy and
# https-mtls from it. A scheme appears here only once the engine module
# that serves it is present, so the list is never ahead of the code.
supported_protocols="@SUPPORT_PROTOCOLS@"
supported_features="@SUPPORT_FEATURES@"

Name: libcurl
URL: https://curl.se/
Description: Library to transfer files with HTTP, FTP, etc.
# CURLVERSION expands to 8.19.0-DEV, read from include/curl/curlver.h:35
# exactly as configure.ac:129 read it, with LIBCURL_VERSION_NUM 0x081300
# at :61 as its numeric twin. It is NOT bumped: the version is part of the
# frozen contract, and every parity claim made about this artifact -- 100
# exported symbols, 103 CURLcode discriminants, 308 CURLoption
# identifiers -- is a claim against curl 8.19.0-DEV.
Version: @CURLVERSION@
# Both of the next two fields expand to EMPTY, and empty is the truthful
# value rather than an omission. Requires: names other pkg-config modules
# and pkg-config fails outright when one of them cannot be found, so a
# stale name here would break `pkg-config --cflags libcurl` for every
# consumer. Every C library curl used to require has become a statically
# linked Rust crate with no .pc file of its own: OpenSSL, GnuTLS, mbedTLS
# and wolfSSL became rustls; nghttp2 became h2; ngtcp2, nghttp3 and quiche
# became quinn, h3 and h3-quinn; libssh2 and libssh became russh; zlib
# became flate2; the C brotli and zstd libraries became the brotli and
# zstd crates; c-ares became the system resolver; libidn2 became idna;
# libpsl became publicsuffix; libuv became tokio. There is nothing left to
# require. CMakeLists.txt:2144 and :2260 leave both empty as well.
Requires: @LIBCURL_PC_REQUIRES@
Requires.private: @LIBCURL_PC_REQUIRES_PRIVATE@
# The link line, and the one literal in this file that must never change:
# consumers link -lcurl. That is why curl-rs-ffi/Cargo.toml:114-126 builds
# the cdylib and the staticlib under [lib] name = "curl", so the artifacts
# really are libcurl.so and libcurl.a rather than libcurl_rs_ffi.so.
#
# The shared object carries DT_SONAME = libcurl.so.4. The 4 is derived,
# never chosen: lib/Makefile.soname sets VERSIONCHANGE=12, VERSIONADD=0
# and VERSIONDEL=8, giving libtool -version-info 12:0:8, whose soname
# major is current - age = 12 - 8 = 4. Cargo stamps no soname of its own,
# and .cargo/config.toml deliberately does not either -- rustflags are not
# artifact-scoped and were measured to put that soname on the two
# command-line binaries as well. curl-rs-ffi/build.rs emits the
# correctly-scoped cdylib-only link argument instead, and an
# -install_name form of it on macOS.
Libs: -L${libdir} -lcurl @LIBCURL_PC_LIBS@
# What a consumer must add when it links the static archive instead. On
# the four mandated targets this is the platform C runtime and nothing
# exotic: -lgcc_s -lutil -lrt -lpthread -lm -ldl -lc on the two
# unknown-linux-gnu triples, and -lSystem -lm with the Security and
# CoreFoundation frameworks on the two apple-darwin ones, plus
# -lgssapi_krb5 or the GSS framework when the `negotiate` feature is on.
# ring, the pinned cryptographic provider, contributes nothing here: its C
# and assembly are compiled into the archive, so there is no external
# library to name. The order is the one a left-to-right static linker
# needs, which is why -lc is last. The same authority renders
# `curl-config --static-libs`, so the two cannot disagree.
Libs.private: @LIBCURL_PC_LDFLAGS_PRIVATE@ @LIBCURL_PC_LIBS_PRIVATE@
# includedir must be the directory that HOLDS the curl subdirectory, not
# the subdirectory itself, because the public headers are included as
# <curl/curl.h> -- see include/README.md. The header found there is now
# generated: curl-rs-ffi/build.rs runs cbindgen over the ABI crate to
# produce include/curl/curl.h, and include/curl/typecheck-gcc.h ships
# verbatim beside it as a hand-maintained file, because its 258 curlcheck_
# macros give curl_easy_setopt compile-time type checking that cbindgen
# cannot express.
Cflags: -I${includedir} @LIBCURL_PC_CFLAGS@
# -DCURL_STATICLIB, exactly as CMakeLists.txt:2255 sets it. A consumer
# linking the static archive must define it so the public headers do not
# decorate the API for import from a shared library.
Cflags.private: @LIBCURL_PC_CFLAGS_PRIVATE@
